### Reasoning

To solve this problem, we need to find a set of n distinct positive numbers a1, a2, ..., an such that GCD(ai, aj) ≠ 1 for any i and j, and GCD(a1, a2, ..., an) = 1.

First, let's consider the condition GCD(ai, aj) ≠ 1. This implies that all ai and aj should have a common factor other than 1. 

Next, we need to ensure that GCD(a1, a2, ..., an) = 1, meaning that there should be no common factor greater than 1 that divides all ai.

One way to achieve this is by selecting the first n prime numbers (p1, p2, ..., pn). If we let ai be the product of all prime numbers except pi, then GCD(ai, aj) will be the product of the prime numbers common to both ai and aj (which is at least the product of all primes except pi and pj, thus not 1), and GCD(a1, a2, ..., an) will be 1 because there is no prime factor common to all ai.

However, upon closer inspection, this initial thought is not entirely accurate because the product of all primes except pi will indeed share a factor with the product of all primes except pj (for i ≠ j), but it won't be just any common factor - it will be the product of all primes except both pi and pj. Still, the idea of using primes is on the right track.

A more accurate approach is to directly find n distinct numbers that satisfy the given conditions. We can achieve this by using the first n primes. For each ai, we can assign it a product involving primes such that every pair (ai, aj) shares a prime factor (thus GCD(ai, aj) ≠ 1), but not all ai share the same prime factor.

For n = 2, we can't satisfy the conditions because for GCD(a1, a2) ≠ 1 and GCD(a1, a2) = 1, it's impossible. For larger n, we can actually use the first n+1 primes to generate the numbers. The idea is to let each ai be a product of n-1 primes out of the first n+1 primes, excluding a different prime each time.

