### Reasoning

To solve this problem, we need to find a subset of the digits in the given number n such that the resulting number is a multiple of 3 and has the minimum number of digits erased. 

First, we need to understand that a number is a multiple of 3 if the sum of its digits is a multiple of 3. So, we will calculate the sum of the digits of the given number n and check its remainder when divided by 3.

We will then try to remove the minimum number of digits to make the sum of the remaining digits a multiple of 3. 

If the sum of the digits is already a multiple of 3, we don't need to erase any digits. 

If the sum of the digits is not a multiple of 3, we will check the remainder of the sum when divided by 3. If the remainder is 1, we will try to remove a digit with a value of 1 modulo 3. If the remainder is 2, we will try to remove a digit with a value of 2 modulo 3.

We also need to ensure that the resulting number does not have leading zeroes. So, we will remove the first digit if it is zero, unless the number is zero itself.

We will consider all possible ways to remove digits to make the sum a multiple of 3 and choose the one that results in the maximum number (i.e., the number with the minimum number of digits erased).

